  "response": "The case of Mercury is usually told as a sequel to the triumph of celestial mechanics in the 1840s. Urbain Le Verrier predicted the position of Neptune from irregularities in the orbit of Uranus, and only afterward did Johann Galle first observe Neptune in 1848, close to the place the calculation had specified. The method was simple in outline: treat an unexplained deviation in a known orbit as the gravitational signature of an unknown mass, then compute where that mass must lie. Its success gave the inference from orbital residuals to hidden bodies an authority that shaped the next half-century of work on the inner solar system.\n\nLe Verrier reported an unexplained excess in the precession of Mercury's perihelion in 1859. That report resulted from a prior commitment: Le Verrier had proposed that an undiscovered planet orbiting inside Mercury's orbit caused the anomaly, and his reduction of the Mercurial data was framed to measure the effect such a body would produce. The hypothetical planet inside Mercury's orbit was given the name Vulcan, a christening that followed directly from the prestige of Galle's sighting, since the discovery of Neptune had made the naming of a predicted world seem a mere formality awaiting confirmation at the telescope.\n\nObservational claims arrived quickly. The amateur astronomer Edmond Lescarbault claimed to have seen an unknown body transit the Sun in 1859, and Le Verrier, after visiting him, endorsed the sighting. The logical situation was nonetheless stark. Either a planet inside Mercury's orbit massive enough to account for the perihelion anomaly exists, or, despite Lescarbault's report, no planet inside Mercury's orbit massive enough to account for the perihelion anomaly exists; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Because a body of the required mass would be bright enough to detect near the solar limb, the dispute could be settled by observation rather than by further calculation. Charles Dillon Perrine's photographic searches at early twentieth-century eclipses found no intra-Mercurial planet, and those plates, which reached faint limiting magnitudes across the region where such a body would have to lie, indicate that the second of the two possibilities is the true one. Because the required planet is absent, the astronomical community ultimately concluded that the planet Vulcan does not exist. That verdict settled the tension created by Lescarbault's transit: whatever he saw, it could not have been a world of the mass the perihelion demanded.\n\nNewtonian gravity departs from the true law of gravitation in the strong solar field near Mercury. Eliminating one candidate cause narrowed the field rather than closing it, and other material explanations remained on the table, including a flattened Sun and diffuse rings of zodiacal dust. At least one of the following holds, perhaps both: unseen matter near the Sun perturbs Mercury's orbit beyond the effects of the known planets, or Einstein's general relativity predicted an excess perihelion advance for Mercury of about 75 arcseconds per century. The quantitative test was decisive in its own terms. General relativity accounts for about 43 arcseconds per century of Mercury's perihelion advance; in other words, Einstein's theory of gravitation explains roughly 43 seconds of arc per century of extra precession in Mercury's perihelion, without any adjustable parameter tuned to the observed value.\n\nWhat distinguished this episode from the search for Neptune was not a change in the logic of inference but a change in what the residual pointed toward. In the Uranian case the anomaly was matched by a body that the telescope then found; in the Mercurian case repeated searches came back empty, while a new theory of gravitation reproduced the discrepancy from first principles. Weighing the null eclipse results against the precision of the relativistic calculation, astronomers ultimately determined that Mercury's anomalous precession arose from a flaw in Newtonian gravity rather than from an unseen planet.",
